I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied PXRD/GSAS-II helpers first. Then I’ll screen candidate phases, audit atomistic models, refine competing complete-pattern branches, and only export the final files after the project and submission audits pass.

The sample is a Cu Kα reflection scan from 9.9–100.1° with Mn₂O₃ and WO₃ precursors heated at 500 °C. Because the product formula and phase count are unspecified, I’m treating precursor remnants and Mn–W–O reaction products as competing hypotheses rather than assuming a single target phase.

The peak inventory strongly favors residual/parent oxides: the dense triplet near 23–25° and multiplets near 33–35°, 50–51°, and 54–56° match low-symmetry WO₃, while the distinct ~33.27°, 38.53°, 45.13°, 49.61°, 66.03°, and 81.83° family is consistent with bixbyite Mn₂O₃. The strongest diagnostic reflections expected for MnWO₄ or Mn₃WO₆ are absent, so I’m carrying the parent oxides and the closest WO₃ polymorphs into matched refinements.

The CIF integrity screen rejects one older P2₁/m WO₃ model because it contains near-duplicate oxygen sites, and flags another P2₁/c entry for implausibly short O–O contacts. I’m excluding those malformed models. The retained comparison set uses audited triclinic P-1 and monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ structures plus audited cubic Ia-3 Mn₂O₃, with identical nuisance-parameter staging.

The first parallel screen exposed a setup issue rather than a scientific one: each branch was writing the same temporary converted pattern, causing a race and incomplete imports. I’ve isolated the working files per branch; the existing branch projects are being discarded, and the matched comparison will be rerun cleanly.

The clean screen is now stable. Monoclinic WO₃ already reaches about 10% Rwp after only background, phase scales, one angular term, and cells; the orthorhombic alternative remains above 22% at the comparable stage. I’m letting the triclinic and profile stages finish before deciding, since the lower symmetry model must earn its added degrees of freedom on the full pattern.

The angular diagnostic is behaving as expected for Bragg–Brentano data: zero and specimen-displacement corrections are nearly interchangeable, and the joint fit is >99.9% correlated, so it will be rejected. The zero-only branch remains the parsimonious baseline; a displacement-only branch has not shown a compensating full-pattern gain.

The minimal Gaussian screen is physically stable in both structural alternatives. Refining U and W gives only a small gain over W alone (about 0.04 Rwp), with positive widths and no profile-term correlation; I’m carrying that two-term state forward while testing X and Y Lorentzian broadening independently. The phase fractions are already stable near 72–73 wt% WO₃ and 27–28 wt% Mn₂O₃ across these branches.

The full-pattern leave-one-out test confirms WO₃ is indispensable: removing it drives Rwp from ~7.3% to ~34.6% despite reconvergence. I’m finishing the Mn₂O₃ leave-out, reaction-product add-ins, and same-composition Mn₂O₃ symmetry replacements before locking the phase inventory.

The phase inventory is now stable: triclinic WO₃ plus cubic Mn₂O₃ is the only supported two-phase model, while manganese tungstates fail their diagnostic peaks and scale tests. I’m finishing matched profile/envelope/texture checks, then I’ll freeze a converged replay-safe GSAS-II project and audit every exported file.

The remaining intensity mismatch is chiefly an angle-dependent envelope effect, not unexplained peak positions. I’m comparing polarization and a one-parameter Suortti roughness correction from the same frozen baseline; this avoids giving either model credit through drifting cells or peak widths.

Polarization gives a large, stable full-pattern improvement (Rwp 7.307% → 6.884%) without problematic correlations. Surface roughness can lower Rwp further, but its parameter is strongly coupled to the WO₃ scale and depends on an arbitrarily fixed roughness fraction, so I am treating it as non-unique rather than adopting it. I’m now checking whether any simple preferred-orientation axis has reproducible support.

The [001] WO₃ March–Dollase term is weak but reproducible (ratio 1.0084(13)) and improves most angular regions; cubic Mn₂O₃ texture branches diverge or couple to scale and are rejected. A matched deletion test also shows the constant Gaussian W term has negligible practical benefit once polarization is modeled, so I’m simplifying the profile to positive U + X and repeating all per-phase size/strain tests from that updated baseline.

The phase-specific broadening tests are now discriminated: Mn₂O₃ size broadening is positive and stable (~0.36 μm), while strain becomes redundant once size is present; both WO₃ terms give only tiny, angle-localized gains and are excluded. I’m now rerunning the required angular branch selector on this mature model before the final freeze.

The mature angular rerun is behaving as expected: the zero-only alternative degrades the fit, and the displacement branch is being reconverged with the accepted Mn₂O₃ broadening active. I’m retaining a branch only if the helper’s stability and correlation checks pass, not merely because its raw Rwp is lower.

The mature angular comparison retained the existing displacement-only calibration: re-optimizing it changed Rwp by only 0.0011%, the zero model was much worse, and the lower-Rwp joint zero/displacement model was rejected at 99.94% correlation. I’m freezing that vetted model now, then exporting directly from the frozen project and running the three required audits.

Before export I’m doing the mandated mature-baseline retest of every source-supported alternative: both manganese tungstates as additions, both Mn₂O₃ symmetry alternatives, and monoclinic WO₃ as replacements. These branches all inherit the same accepted background, displacement, profile, polarization, texture, and Mn₂O₃ size model.

One retest changed the inventory question: MnWO₄ now refines to 0.61(9) wt% and lowers Rwp by 0.021%, whereas Mn₃WO₆ remains only 0.22(11) wt% with negligible benefit. I’m therefore treating MnWO₄ as a live trace-phase candidate and checking whether its gain comes from several unique peaks—especially the nominal 18.5° and 30° diagnostics—rather than overlap credit.

The trace candidate passes the qualitative peak test: it improves distinct residuals near 15.8°, 18.7°, 30.3°, 30.7°, 41.5°, 53.8°, and 65.2°, with gains across several angular regions. Its cell cannot be refined stably at this abundance, so I’m retaining the audited literature MnWO₄ cell and will report the ~0.6 wt% fraction as less secure than the two major phases. The complete three-phase model now gets the same nuisance and leave-one-out retesting as the major-phase model.

A technical profile check exposed an important GSAS-II default: even “unrefined” HAP size = 1 μm and microstrain = 1000 still broaden peaks. I’m correcting the baseline to physically neutral values (very large size, zero strain), re-optimizing the global profile, and then repeating size/strain tests. This can change the optional broadening terms and their uncertainties, but not the phase identities.

The corrected neutral-broadening baseline is stable. Six matched branches are now running—size and strain independently for WO₃, Mn₂O₃, and trace MnWO₄. They are intentionally slower because each regenerates the complete pattern; no branch will be accepted unless it converges with a positive parameter and improves more than one angular region.

I’ve narrowed the model to triclinic WO₃, cubic Mn₂O₃, and a trace wolframite MnWO₄ component. I’m finishing the matched profile-branch tests now, then I’ll freeze and audit the exact GSAS-II project and exports.

The joint test confirms Mn₂O₃ size and strain are redundant: once strain is present, the fitted crystallite-size broadening collapses to zero within uncertainty. I’m retaining only the smaller, stable isotropic-strain model and checking that its gain is distributed across the pattern.

The staged nuisance terms have reconverged cleanly: polarization 0.662, a very small WO₃ [001] March correction (1.008), positive global U/X widths, and Mn₂O₃ microstrain near 1.0×10³ µstrain. No >0.9 parameter correlations remain. I’m now rerunning the required angular-offset competition from this complete three-phase baseline.

The angular test is behaving as expected so far: forcing a zero-only correction degrades the full-pattern fit sharply, while the existing specimen-displacement model remains near the mature baseline. The helper is still completing the correlated joint branch before it makes the selection.

The angular helper selected the mature displacement-only baseline: refining displacement changes Rwp by only 0.0005 points, while zero-only is much worse and the lower-Rwp joint model is rejected for 99.94% zero/displacement correlation. I’m preserving that stable baseline and running the final leave-one-out/replacement checks from it.

The mature replacement tests are decisive: P‑1 WO₃ and Ia‑3 Mn₂O₃ outperform P2₁/c WO₃ and both I2₁3/Pbca Mn₂O₃ alternatives by large whole-pattern margins. Removing MnWO₄ worsens Rwp from 6.8363% to 6.8552%. A fourth Mn₃WO₆ trial gives only a 0.0036-point gain at ~0.31 wt%, so I’m checking whether that gain has genuinely unique multi-peak support or is overlap fitting.

The matched removals already show the Gaussian U term is essential (Rwp rises to 7.64% even after X is reoptimized). I’m letting the complementary X-removal branch converge before testing polarization, texture, and Mn₂O₃ strain one at a time.

All retained optional terms survive matched removal: polarization (Rwp 7.254%), WO₃ texture (6.853%), Mn₂O₃ strain (6.845%), U (7.640%), and X (10.654%) versus 6.836% baseline. The Mn₃WO₆ gain is overlap-dominated—its strongest predicted peaks often worsen local residuals—so I’m rejecting that fourth phase and doing the final unretained size/strain retest before freezing.

The final retest rejects WO₃ size/strain (effectively zero within uncertainty) and MnWO₄ size (boundary/divergent). MnWO₄ strain finds an extreme ~2% value with >50% relative uncertainty and doubles the trace fraction; I’m giving that branch extra cycles and a range-by-range check before deciding whether it is physically identifiable or just a broad-background surrogate.

The extra cycles make the MnWO₄ strain branch stable, positive, and beneficial in five of six angle regions. Its strain is large and only ~2σ (2.04(1.05)%), with moderate scale correlation (0.76), so I’m retaining it as the smallest supported trace-phase profile term but will flag the resulting MnWO₄ fraction as systematically uncertain. I’m reconverging the full nuisance model around that choice now.

The retained trace-strain model has reconverged at Rwp 6.8291% with Mn₂O₃ strain 1091(211) µstrain and MnWO₄ strain 2.08(1.03)%. I’m rerunning the angular selector once more because this accepted downstream change can shift the offset competition; after that, only final phase leave-outs and export audits remain.

The last inventory pass confirms MnWO₄ is necessary (removal raises Rwp to 6.8554%). Mn₃WO₆ remains below the preset material-improvement margin (6.8291→6.8243%) and still lacks unique-peak support, so it stays excluded. I’m freezing all accepted nuisance terms and refining only background/scales into the submitted `final.gpx`.

`final.gpx` is now replay-safe and passes the numerical/model audit: converged, no dropped variables or warnings, exact regenerated-profile agreement, and positive total widths for all three phases. I’m exporting CIFs directly from this audited project, then I’ll build the cross-checked profile/result/report files and run the submission audit.

